<Layout 
  title="Maimonides in Order — Elie Schulman" 
  description="A chronological Maimonides book series with coordinated Scholarly, General Public, and Children's editions."
>
<header class="page-header">
  <div class="page-header-inner">
    <p class="eyebrow">Maimonides in Order</p>
    <h1>One source sequence. Three ways in.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Sixteen ebook positions follow Maimonides' writings chronologically. Each position is developed in Scholarly, General Public, and Children's registers without changing the source order.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="content">
  <h2>Choose a register</h2>
  <div class="featured-grid">
    <div class="featured-card">
      <h4>Scholarly</h4>
      <p class="featured-type">Primary texts · Hebrew and Arabic · close reading</p>
      <p class="note">For readers who enter through the source and want the philosophical and textual apparatus.</p>
      <p><a class="featured-link" href="/maimonides/scholarly/">Enter →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="featured-card">
      <h4>General Public</h4>
      <p class="featured-type">Same source spine · direct, accessible register</p>
      <p class="note">For readers who enter through a question, scene, or provocation.</p>
      <p><a class="featured-link" href="/maimonides/general/">Enter →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="featured-card">
      <h4>Children</h4>
      <p class="featured-type">Same source spine · concrete language and imagery</p>
      <p class="note">The difficulty remains; the language changes.</p>
      <p><a class="featured-link" href="/maimonides/children/">Enter →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Current development</h2>
  <div class="resource-card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Approved matrix · 17 chapters · Chapters 1–2 approved</p>
    <h3>Emes/Truth — Yesodei HaTorah 1:1–1:4</h3>
    <p><em>Emet</em> is treated as ontological—genuine, non-contingent existence—not merely the accuracy of a statement. The recovered audience files preserve Chapter 2 components; Chapter 1 remains a separately preserved source component pending an explicitly documented assembly.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="resource-card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Approved matrix · 4 chapters · drafting not begun</p>
    <h3>If He Does Not Exist — Yesodei HaTorah 1:2</h3>
    <p>A phenomenological reading of dependence rather than a simple first-cause argument. The matrix is preserved; no chapter prose is presented as complete.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="resource-card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Approved revised matrix · 4 chapters · drafting not begun</p>
    <h3>If the Foundation Vanished — Yesodei HaTorah 1:5–1:6</h3>
    <p>Divine oneness is unlike every worldly instance of “one”: neither category nor body, with incorporeality as the logical ground of unity.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="resource-card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Approved draft · 8 chapters in each register</p>
    <h3>Ibn Tibbon's Preface</h3>
    <p>Three complete audience drafts examine the translator's difficulty and method. This translation-process project remains distinct from the ontological argument of <em>Emes</em>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The sixteen-position map</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Treatise on Logic, Chapter 10 — kinds of predication</li>
    <li>Treatise on Logic, Chapter 14 — inner and outer speech</li>
    <li>Shemoneh Perakim — the soul and its names</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 1:1–1:4 — <em>Emes/Truth</em></li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 1:5–1:6 — divine unity</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 1:7–1:12 — unity, incorporeality, and accommodated language</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 2:1–2:2 — love, awe, and contemplation</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 2:3 — three created categories</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 2:4–2:9 — angels, ranks, and knowledge</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 2:10 — knower, known, and knowledge</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 4:7 — the indescribable</li>
    <li>Yesodei HaTorah 4:8 — false dichotomies</li>
    <li>Guide Introduction and I:1, I:50–57, I:64–65 — limits of utterance</li>
    <li>Guide I:46, I:64–65 — soul-to-soul communication</li>
    <li>Letter to Ibn Tibbon — the translation rule</li>
    <li>Ibn Tibbon's glossary — why translation requires apparatus</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Existing series remain distinct</h2>
  <p>The <a class="work-link" href="/maimonides/foundations/">Foundations Ebook Project</a> preserves twenty-two completed law-specific volumes. <a class="work-link" href="/maimonides/books/">Books &amp; Manuscripts</a> and <a class="work-link" href="/maimonides/essays/">standalone essays</a> retain their own formats and histories. The three-audience structure is not imposed on every Maimonides publication.</p>
</div>
</Layout>
